      <floats-group id="article-19388.s17">
        <fig id="article-19388.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>X-Ray, Blunt Chest Trauma. A frontal chest radiograph shows a hollow viscus in the left hemithorax with a mediastinal shift to the right. A nasogastric tube terminates in the thorax, suggesting diaphragmatic rupture with herniation of abdominal contents. Contributed by H Shulman, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Case_01" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-19388.s18">
        <fig id="article-19388.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Blunt Chest Trauma on Computed Tomography. Blunt chest trauma caused organ rupture within the thoracic cavity. CT imaging shows bowel herniation into the left hemithorax with mediastinal shift to the right. A small amount of hyperattenuating fluid is seen adjacent to the liver and spleen, consistent with hemoperitoneum. Findings are compatible with a traumatic diaphragmatic rupture. Contributed by H Shulman, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="4in1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
